==English==

===Etymology===
{{ety|en|:inh|enm:confounden|tree=1}}
{{root|en|ine-pro|*ǵʰewd-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|confounden|t=destroy, ruin, perplex}}, from {{der|en|xno|cunfundre}} and {{der|en|fro|confondre}}<!--glossed as "destroy", but neither the Latin source nor the English borrowing has that sense-->, from {{der|en|la|cōnfundō|t=to mingle, mix together}}. Related to {{m|en|found||to melt (metals in a {{l|en|foundry}})|id=melt}} (but not to {{m|en|found||to start|id=start}}, nor to {{m|en|find}}) and to {{m|en|fusion}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/kənˈfaʊnd/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-confound.ogg|a=US}}
*: {{rhymes|en|aʊnd|s=2}}
* {{hyph|en|con|found}}

===Verb===
{{wikidata lexeme|L331220}}
{{en-verb}}

# To [[perplex]] or [[puzzle]].
#: {{syn|en|puzzle|vex}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1830|author=w:Joseph Smith, Jr.|title=[[s:Book of Mormon (1981)/Ether|Book of Mormon: Ether]]|section=i, 34
|passage=And the brother of Jared being a large and mighty man, and a man highly favored of the Lord, Jared, his brother, said unto him: Cry unto the Lord, that he will not '''confound''' us that we may not understand our words.}}
#* {{RQ:Guardian|author=Kevin Mitchell|title=Roger Federer back from Wimbledon 2012 brink to beat {{w|Julien Benneteau}}|url=https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2012/jun/29/roger-federer-wimbledon-2012-julien-benneteau|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20161115072742/https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2012/jun/29/roger-federer-wimbledon-2012-julien-benneteau|archivedate=15 November 2016|date=29 June 2012|passage=The fightback when it came was in the [[w:Roger Federer|[Roger] Federer]] fashion: unfussy, filled with classy strokes from the back with perfectly timed interventions at the net that '''confounded''' his opponent. The third set passed in a bit of a blur, the fourth, which led to the second tie-break, was the most dramatic of the match.}}
# To [[stun]] or [[amaze]].
# To fail to see the [[difference]]; to mix up; to confuse [[right]] and [[wrong]].
#: {{syn|en|confuse|mix up|Thesaurus:confuse}}
#* {{RQ:Hobbes Government|chapter=XIV|chaptername=Of Lawes and Trespasses|page=210|passage=They vvho leſſe ſeriouſly conſider the force of vvords, doe ſometimes '''confound''' Lavv vvith ''Counſell'', ſometimes vvith ''Covenant'', ſometimes vvith ''Right''. They '''confound''' ''Lavv'' vvith ''Counſel'', vvho think, that it is the duty of Monarchs not onely to give ear to their ''Counſellours'', but alſo to obey them, as though it vvere in vaine to take ''Counſell'', unleſſe it vvere alſo follovved.}}
# {{lb|en|sometimes|proscribed}} To make something worse.<!--by confusion with "compound the problem"??-->
#: {{ux|en|Don't '''confound''' the situation by yelling.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1983|author=Carol M. Anderson; Susan Stewart|title=Mastering Resistance: A Practical Guide to Family Therapy
|passage=While she had obeyed him, smiling sweetly all the time, she had nursed a growing resentment of what she called his "Latin American ''macho'' attitude." To '''confound''' the problem, his mother, who lived with them on and off, was described by the wife as being as domineering as her son.}}
# To [[combine]] in a confused fashion; to [[mingle]] so as to make the parts indistinguishable.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1611|author=[[w:Thomas Coryat|Coryat]]|title=Crudities|volume=I|page=195|text=There the freſh and ſalt water would meete and be '''confounded''' together, {{...}}|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/dli.granth.72523/page/195/mode/1up}}
# To cause to be [[ashamed]]; to [[abash]].
#: {{ux|en|His actions '''confounded''' the skeptics.}}
# To [[defeat]], to [[frustrate]], to [[thwart]].
#* {{RQ:King James Version|1 Corinthians|1|27|passage=But God hath choſen the fooliſh things of the woꝛld, to '''confound''' the wiſe: and God hath choſen the weake things of the woꝛld, to '''confound''' the things which are mighty:}}
# {{lb|en|dated}} To [[damn]] (''a mild oath'').
#: {{ux|en|'''Confound''' you!}}
#: {{ux|en|'''Confound''' the lady!}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1882|author=w:Arthur Conan Doyle|chapter=[[s:The Gully of Bluemansdyke and Other Stories/My Friend the Murderer|My Friend the Murderer]]|title=The Gully of Bluemansdyke and Other Stories
|passage="Number 43 is no better, Doctor," said the head-warder, in a slightly reproachful accent, looking in round the corner of my door.<br>"'''Confound''' 43!" I responded from behind the pages of the ''Australian Sketcher''.}}
#* {{RQ:Sewell Black Beauty|page=107|text="'''Confound''' these bearing reins!" he said to himself; "I thought we should have some mischief soon—master will be sorely vexed;{{nb...}}"}}
# {{lb|en|archaic}} To [[destroy]], [[ruin]], or [[devastate]]; to bring to [[ruination]].
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=I|lines=51–53|passage=To mortal men, he with his horrid crew / Lay vanquiſht, rowling in the fiery Gulfe / '''Confounded''' though immortal: But his doom{{...}}|year=1873}}
#* {{RQ:Swift Gulliver|part=IV|page=345|passage= Imagine twenty thouſand of them breaking into the midſt of an ''European'' Army, '''confounding''' the Ranks, overturning the Carriages, battering the Warriors Faces into Mummy, by terrible Yerks from their hinder Hoofs.}}

